# XCU sh-functions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5/none of the expansions
class      required
says       A function definition stores the body without expanding it; each
           call expands and runs it afresh.
test       t/001-define-call.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5/The operands to the command temporarily
class      required
says       A function's operands become its positional parameters and $#; $0
           is unchanged; the caller's parameters come back afterwards.
test       t/002-parameters.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5/Exit Status
class      required
says       A function call has the status of the last command run in it; the
           status of a definition is zero.
test       t/003-status.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5
class      required
says       The body of a function may be any compound command: a subshell,
           if, for, case or while.
test       t/004-compound-bodies.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5/optional <i>io-redirect</i> redirections
class      required
says       Redirections written after a function definition apply at each
           call, not at the definition.
test       t/005-redirect-at-call.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5/separate name
class      required
says       Functions and variables have separate name spaces.
test       t/006-separate-names.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5/The operands to the command temporarily
class      required
says       A function runs in the current environment: variables it sets
           stay, and shift in it changes only its own parameters.
test       t/007-env-effects.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.5
class      required
says       A function can call itself, and a later definition replaces an
           earlier one.
test       t/008-recursion-redefine.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-functions.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.13/Changes made to the subshell environment
class      required
says       A function defined in a subshell is not defined in the shell that
           started it.
test       t/009-subshell-scope.sh
